@startuml Basic Factory Pattern

' Define the styles
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam classFontStyle bold
skinparam classBackgroundColor #f8f9fa
skinparam classBorderColor #dee2e6
skinparam arrowColor #6c757d
skinparam stereotypeCBackgroundColor #e9ecef

' Title
title Basic Factory Pattern - Core Structure

' Factory class
class Factory <<Factory>> {
  +createProduct(type: String): Product
}

' Product interface/base class
interface Product <<Product>> {
  +operation(): void
}

' Concrete Products
class ConcreteProductA <<ConcreteProduct>> {
  +operation(): void
}

class ConcreteProductB <<ConcreteProduct>> {
  +operation(): void
}

class ConcreteProductC <<ConcreteProduct>> {
  +operation(): void
}

' Relationships
Factory ..> Product : creates >
Product <|.. ConcreteProductA
Product <|.. ConcreteProductB
Product <|.. ConcreteProductC

' Client
class Client <<Client>> {
  -product: Product
  +useProduct(): void
}

Client ..> Factory : uses >
Client o--> Product : depends on >

' Pattern component notes
note right of Factory
  <b>Factory (Creator)</b>

  Responsible for creating product objects.
  Centralizes object creation logic and
  shields clients from instantiation details.

  <b>Key Method:</b>
  createProduct(type) uses conditional
  logic to determine which concrete
  product to instantiate:

  <i>if (type == "A") return new ConcreteProductA()</i>
  <i>else if (type == "B") return new ConcreteProductB()</i>
  <i>else if (type == "C") return new ConcreteProductC()</i>

  Returns Product interface, not
  concrete types.
end note

note left of Product
  <b>Product (Abstract Product)</b>

  Common interface for all objects the
  factory can create. Defines operations
  that all concrete products must implement.

  Clients depend on this abstraction,
  not on concrete product classes.

  Can be an interface or abstract class.
end note

note left of ConcreteProductA
  <b>Concrete Product A</b>

  Implements the Product interface
  with specific behavior.

  Created by Factory when client
  requests type "A".

  Each concrete product represents
  a different variant or implementation.
end note

note right of Client
  <b>Client</b>

  Uses Factory to create products
  instead of instantiating them directly.

  Depends only on Product interface,
  not on concrete implementations.

  <b>Usage Pattern:</b>
  product = factory.createProduct("A")
  product.operation()

  Benefits from loose coupling and
  doesn't need to know which concrete
  class is being used.
end note

' Pattern flow note
note right of Product
  <b>Factory Pattern Flow:</b>

  1. Client calls factory.createProduct(type)
  2. Factory examines the type parameter
  3. Factory instantiates appropriate ConcreteProduct
  4. Factory returns object as Product interface
  5. Client uses product through interface methods

  <b>Key Advantage:</b>
  Client code remains unchanged when new
  product types are added to the factory.
end note

' Legend
note right of Product
  <b>Factory Pattern Core Components:</b>

  <b>Factory:</b> Creates product objects based
  on input parameters or conditions. Encapsulates
  instantiation logic.

  <b>Product (Interface):</b> Common interface
  for all objects that factory creates.

  <b>ConcreteProduct:</b> Specific implementations
  of the Product interface. Each represents a
  different type or variant.

  <b>Client:</b> Uses factory to obtain products.
  Depends on Product interface, not concrete classes.

  <b>Key Benefits:</b>
  • Encapsulates object creation
  • Reduces coupling (client doesn't know concrete types)
  • Centralizes instantiation logic
  • Provides meaningful names for creation operations
  • Makes code easier to test (can mock factory)

  <b>Intent:</b>
  Define an interface for creating objects, but
  let the factory decide which class to instantiate.
  Encapsulates object creation to promote loose coupling.
end note

@enduml